  "id": "Compound::DB01213 - Gene::124",
  "verbalization": "Fomepizole is known to bind to Adh1b and Adh1c, which are isoforms of the alcohol dehydrogenase (ADH) enzyme. These isoforms are involved in the catalytic activity of ADH, a critical metabolic pathway for the conversion of alcohol to acetaldehyde. The observed binding of Fomepizole to Adh1b and Adh1c suggests a potential mechanism by which the drug may inhibit ADH activity, thereby reducing the metabolism of alcohol. This inhibition is further supported by the fact that both Adh1b and Adh1c are involved in the same metabolic pathway, Adh1a, which is a key enzyme in the alcohol metabolism process. The integrated evidence from the three paths indicates that Fomepizole may act as a competitive inhibitor of ADH activity, targeting multiple isoforms that ultimately contribute to the function of Adh1a. The consistent binding interactions and the shared participation in ADH activity suggest a plausible mechanism of action, although the exact extent of inhibition and its clinical relevance require further investigation.",
